 and Y, standing on opposite sides of the car.">
        <button type="button" aria-label="Click to enlarge image of Sound waves coming out of a car stopped on a road are shown as spherical areas of compression. The waves are shown to reach two observers, X and Y, standing on opposite sides of the car." class="image-button-wrapper"><img src="/apps/image-cdn/v1/f=webp/apps/archive/20260604.144757/resources/9b5305b1e16826a97ba6aa70cbda2eefd8da2ebf" data-media-type="image/jpg" alt="Sound waves coming out of a car stopped on a road are shown as spherical areas of compression. The waves are shown to reach two observers, X and Y, standing on opposite sides of the car." width="700" height="447" loading="lazy" data-original-src="/apps/archive/20260604.144757/resources/9b5305b1e16826a97ba6aa70cbda2eefd8da2ebf"></button>
      </span>

    <figcaption class="os-caption-container">
  <span class="os-title-label">Figure </span>
<span class="os-number">17.15</span>



  <span class="os-divider"> </span>
  <span class="os-caption">Sounds emitted by a source spread out in spherical waves. Because the source, observers, and air are stationary, the wavelength and frequency are the same in all directions and to all observers.</span>
</figcaption></figure></div><div class="os-figure" id="import-auto-id1999554"><figure data-id="import-auto-id1999554" class="ui-has-child-figcaption"><span data-type="media" id="import-auto-id1427043" data-alt="Two observers X and Y are standing at two ends of a road. A car is shown to move from observer X on the left toward observer Y on the right. The sound waves are shown as spherical air compressions spreading out from points from which they are emitted marked from one through five. The air compressions are shown to arrive more frequently for the observer Y toward whom the car moves, compared to the compressions reaching X.">
        <button type="button" aria-label="Click to enlarge image of Two observers X and Y are standing at two ends of a road. A car is shown to move from observer X on the left toward observer Y on the right. The sound waves are shown as spherical air compressions spreading out from points from which they are emitted marked from one through five. The air compressions are shown to arrive more frequently for the observer Y toward whom the car moves, compared to the compressions reaching X." class="image-button-wrapper"><img src="/apps/image-cdn/v1/f=webp/apps/archive/20260604.144757/resources/d87ccd481d71c20cf47f452a044b3fc696125da7" data-media-type="image/jpg" alt="Two observers X and Y are standing at two ends of a road. A car is shown to move from observer X on the left toward observer Y on the right. The sound waves are shown as spherical air compressions spreading out from points from which they are emitted marked from one through five. The air compressions are shown to arrive more frequently for the observer Y toward whom the car moves, compared to the compressions reaching X." width="700" height="447" loading="lazy" data-original-src="/apps/archive/20260604.144757/resources/d87ccd481d71c20cf47f452a044b3fc696125da7"></button>
      </span>

    <figcaption class="os-caption-container">
  <span class="os-title-label">Figure </span>
<span class="os-number">17.16</span>



  <span class="os-divider"> </span>
  <span class="os-caption">Sounds emitted by a source moving to the right spread out from the points at which they were emitted. The wavelength is reduced and, consequently, the frequency is increased in the direction of motion, so that the observer on the right hears a higher-pitch sound. The opposite is true for the observer on the left, where the wavelength is increased and the frequency is reduced.</span>
</figcaption></figure></div><div class="os-figure" id="import-auto-id2449194"><figure data-id="import-auto-id2449194" class="ui-has-child-figcaption"><span data-type="media" id="import-auto-id3085341" data-alt="A car is shown stopped on a road. Two observers are shown crossing the road from behind the car. The observer X on the left is moving away from the car, and observer Y on the right is approaching the car. The sound waves coming from a point in the car are shown as spherical air compressions that reach the observers at different frequencies.">
        <button type="button" aria-label="Click to enlarge image of A car is shown stopped on a road. Two observers are shown crossing the road from behind the car. The observer X on the left is moving away from the car, and observer Y on the right is approaching the car. The sound waves coming from a point in the car are shown as spherical air compressions that reach the observers at different frequencies." class="image-button-wrapper"><img src="/apps/image-cdn/v1/f=webp/apps/archive/20260604.144757/resources/97a1104895510ab7849fa7cdc8fb8f8975faafa4" data-media-type="image/jpg" alt="A car is shown stopped on a road. Two observers are shown crossing the road from behind the car. The observer X on the left is moving away from the car, and observer Y on the right is approaching the car. The sound waves coming from a point in the car are shown as spherical air compressions that reach the observers at different frequencies." width="700" height="452" loading="lazy" data-original-src="/apps/archive/20260604.144757/resources/97a1104895510ab7849fa7cdc8fb8f8975faafa4"></button>
      </span>

    <figcaption class="os-caption-container">
  <span class="os-title-label">Figure </span>
<span class="os-number">17.17</span>



  <span class="os-divider"> </span>
  <span class="os-caption">The same effect is produced when the observers move relative to the source. Motion toward the source increases frequency as the observer on the right passes through more wave crests than she would if stationary. Motion away from the source decreases frequency as the observer on the left passes through fewer wave crests than he would if stationary.</span>
</figcaption></figure></div><p id="import-auto-id2678168">We know that wavelength and frequency are related by <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>w</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">=</mo><mi fontstyle="italic">fλ</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>w</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">=</mo><mi fontstyle="italic">fλ</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{v rSub { size 8{w} } =fλ} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span>, where <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>w</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>w</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{v rSub { size 8{w} } } {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> is the fixed speed of sound. The sound moves in a medium and has the same speed <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>w</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>w</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{v rSub { size 8{w} } } {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> in that medium whether the source is moving or not. Thus <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{f} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> multiplied by <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow